Native service migration
========================

Historical migration notes
--------------------------

The procedures below describe the earlier Docker-based migrations. Their
helpers are no longer installed on the Podman system and must not be run
against live Podman data. The scripts and migration backups are retained for
reference; recovering Docker requires restoring a suitable earlier NixOS
generation and coordinating a separate application-data rollback.

LuckPerms now reaches PostgreSQL at 10.245.0.1:5432 through the
pelican-pod0 bridge. The native database still listens only on its Unix
socket and authenticates clients as described below.

LuckPerms game database recovery
-------------------------------

The original shared-MariaDB migration selected FreshRSS and Panel explicitly;
it did not migrate the game database s14_minecraft. Its original data remains
in /var/lib/mariadb. server/luckperms.nix declares its dedicated PostgreSQL
database/owner, SCRAM authentication and daily backup.

The native PostgreSQL server still listens only on its Unix socket. A
systemd socket proxy listens on 172.18.0.1:5432, bound to the pelican0 bridge
device only, and forwards the PostgreSQL protocol to that socket. Clients
must authenticate with the dedicated database password; PostgreSQL is not
bound to the LAN/public interfaces.

After building the system, run its luckperms-migrate helper with --system
and the full built system store path. It clones the stopped, retained
MariaDB directory, starts recovery MariaDB on a private Unix socket with
networking/events/replication disabled, and saves a private source dump.
It uses the installed LuckPerms JAR's PostgreSQL schema, converts permission
booleans, preserves every value and source next ID, and rehearses the import
in isolated PostgreSQL. The empty MariaDB messenger table is not recreated:
PostgreSQL LuckPerms uses LISTEN/NOTIFY instead.

Only after rehearsal does it activate the system, stop Velocity container
ae52fc19-aa2d-4b93-850a-0fc31766c961, restore into an empty PostgreSQL database,
verify all values/constraints/sequences, and replace that server's LuckPerms
connection settings. Applying the new PostgreSQL authentication configuration
briefly restarts PostgreSQL; Docker/Wings and other game containers are not
restarted. It explicitly refreshes the target PostgreSQL configuration after
activation, avoiding an old-unit startup race that can retain the old
authentication file. Since hba_file is a startup-only parameter, an explicit
PostgreSQL restart is required if the target path still has pending_restart.
Local H2 databases and LuckPerms JSON exports remain untouched.
The config backup, source dump, converted dump and recovery state are retained
under /var/backups/luckperms/<timestamp>, readable only by root. The live
plugin config is readable by its original owner/group, not world-readable.

It verifies JDBC access from inside the game container using the installed
PostgreSQL driver, checks actual LuckPerms startup, and saves the system
profile/boot configuration only after success. On a failure after the import,
fix the error and use --finish /var/backups/luckperms/<timestamp> with the
corrected helper. If the game has not started yet, this rechecks the full
import before changing its connection settings; if it has started, it
restarts only Velocity for a clean startup check and verifies persistence.
Neither mode restores over live permissions.
Do not rerun an import or roll back the database after players can write.

Additional LuckPerms servers
---------------------------

The luckperms-servers helper targets the four Bukkit volume UUIDs listed in
luckperms-servers.py. --audit locates existing containers by volume mount,
briefly stops only the H2 server if running, exports a read-only private copy
using its installed H2 driver, then restores its prior running state. It
reports additive rows and conflicts without changing PostgreSQL or configs.
Absent containers are valid and remain uncreated.

After reviewing the audit, --apply --keep-shared-players performs an offline
cutover. All four targets must be stopped or absent; it refuses running
targets rather than starting or restarting anything. It re-exports H2,
refuses any permission or track conflicts, and keeps existing PostgreSQL
player records when username/primary-group fields differ. Missing player
records and nonconflicting permissions are still added. Permission contexts,
negative values and expiry timestamps are retained. History is merged as a
multiset, without duplicating already imported entries.

Each run keeps original configs, a full pre-merge PostgreSQL dump, H2 copy
and private merge plan under /var/backups/luckperms-servers/<timestamp>.
The additive transaction locks all LuckPerms tables, rejects a stale plan,
uses fresh serial IDs and verifies the exact original-plus-added rows before
commit. It never restores over live data. The four configs reuse the working
Velocity PostgreSQL credentials, preserve unrelated settings and ownership,
and are replaced atomically with mode 0640. Original H2 files remain intact.
The helper tests password authentication through the bridge TCP proxy.

If a later config update fails, fix the reported error and rerun: the merge
is idempotent and preserves the original player conflict policy. Do not
restore the database backup after clients can write. Run `lpv sync` in the
running Velocity console after merging to refresh its cached permissions.
Start the Bukkit servers normally through Panel when wanted; their actual
plugin startup can only be verified once they are started.

Native monitoring
-----------------

Gatus is free/open-source software under Apache 2.0. server/monitoring.nix
configures its monitors declaratively: eleven running application health
checks and SSH, every minute. It runs as a static unprivileged gatus account,
uses peer authentication to its native PostgreSQL database over a Unix socket,
and receives daily database backups. It listens only on 127.0.0.1:3001 behind
https://uptime.pulsar.ltd; spacebar is not used for monitoring.

These are local application checks, not external DNS/TLS availability probes.
No notification destinations have been configured. Old Kuma monitoring
history is not imported.

After building the system, activate, verify all twelve monitors have recorded
successful results in PostgreSQL, persist the boot configuration, and delete
the explicitly retired Kuma-only data with:

  sudo /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/gatus-transition \
    --system /etc/nixos/result-native-migration --delete-kuma-data

Cleanup removes /etc/docker-compose/UptimeKuma/data, /var/lib/uptime-kuma and
/var/lib/private/uptime-kuma, plus Kuma-only application directories, SQL dumps
and row-count files inside timestamped native-services/shared-mariadb backups
and their unfinished-native-copies subdirectories. It retains all other
application backups and shared multi-application database recovery directories,
which can still contain historical Kuma tables. A deletion manifest remains
under /var/lib/gatus-transition. Cleanup happens only after monitoring passes.
Old backup manifests are adjusted when a Kuma-only SQL dump is removed.
The helper runs the target system's idempotent PostgreSQL setup as postgres
before activation, so Gatus cannot race an old setup unit during the switch.
This provisions the new role/database without restarting database clients.
If activation fails, no Kuma cleanup or boot-profile update takes place;
correct the failure and rerun the command.
The first-stage migration/rollback tools are historical and must not be used
to restore Kuma after this cleanup.

Grimmory 3.5.0 still needs its separate MariaDB: its shipped Java application
contains the MariaDB JDBC driver and MySQL Flyway plugin/migrations, not a
PostgreSQL driver or PostgreSQL migration set. A connection-string change or
data-only conversion does not make that application PostgreSQL-compatible.

Authentik and Panel OAuth
------------------------

Both authentik.pulsar.ltd proxy routes (including the embedded outpost) use
the host's HTTP port 9180, not container ports 9000/9443. TLS terminates at
Nginx. Authentik remains containerized with native PostgreSQL storage.

The Pelican Panel application has slug pelican and a confidential OAuth
provider allowing only the authorization-code grant and this strict callback:

  https://panel.pulsar.ltd/auth/oauth/callback/authentik

It uses explicit consent and the openid, email and profile scope mappings.
Panel's existing client credentials remain in its runtime .env, never in Nix
configuration or the store. Its automatic account creation and email-based
account linking remain disabled; existing users link Authentik explicitly
from their authenticated Panel profile. Local Panel login remains available.
Provider/application configuration is stored in the backed-up Authentik
database, and Panel user links are stored in the backed-up Panel database.

Shared MariaDB retirement (second stage)
---------------------------------------

FreshRSS remains in its matching-version Docker image but now uses native
PostgreSQL through /run/postgresql with a dedicated SCRAM-authenticated role.
Pelican Panel uses native PostgreSQL with peer authentication for its own
non-superuser account. APP_KEY and all unrelated application settings remain
unchanged. Both databases receive the existing daily PostgreSQL backups.

MariaDB represents JSON as text, so pgloader alone does not reproduce Panel's
PostgreSQL JSON schema. The migration explicitly converts the twenty JSON
columns declared by the installed Panel migrations before application checks
and dump/restore. It preserves their exact text values, including duplicates
and NULL, and tests the notification JSON query used by authenticated pages.
For an already migrated database, run the newly built helper with
--repair-panel-json (without --system). This takes a full PostgreSQL backup
under /var/backups/pelican-json, then converts and checks every affected value
in a single transaction. Invalid JSON, unexpected types or lock timeouts abort
the entire repair. Existing application data is never reimported.

Uptime Kuma is retired because its installed version does not support
PostgreSQL. Its state, MariaDB database, and original container data are
retained, not deleted. Its former homepage returns HTTP 410. No monitoring
replacement is installed automatically. Grimmory's separate MariaDB is
unaffected, as are Wings and game containers.

Build the configuration with path:/etc/nixos as below, then run:

  sudo /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/shared-mariadb-migrate \
    --system /etc/nixos/result-native-migration

Do not run an ordinary switch before this command. FreshRSS and every Pelican
writer/deployment unit require /var/lib/shared-mariadb-migration/ready so they
cannot start against unpopulated PostgreSQL databases.

This command refuses existing destination databases or prior migration state,
and any Docker/containerd/Wings supervisor change. No game restart is needed.
It stops FreshRSS, Panel workers/web/scheduler and Kuma, takes private backups
under /var/backups/shared-mariadb/<timestamp>, and imports into an isolated,
Unix-socket-only PostgreSQL cluster first. pgloader errors are fatal even if it
returns exit status zero. Explicit bigint and FreshRSS tinyint casts preserve
foreign-key compatibility and the application's supported PostgreSQL types.
Unsigned bigint range checks reject overflow rather than truncating.

Every cell in every table is compared via counted SHA-256 row fingerprints,
including duplicate rows, NULL vs empty strings, full UTF8MB4 Unicode, binary
data, and UTC timestamps. All source foreign keys must exist and validate;
source AUTO_INCREMENT next values are explicitly restored and checked. Both
installed applications are booted against copies and the isolated database,
and Panel's migrate command must succeed without changing its migration ledger.
FreshRSS IDs are preserved rather than reassigned by the SQLite transfer tool.

The shared MariaDB is then stopped and its entire data directory backed up,
including retained historical Forgejo and Kuma databases. It is removed from
the target configuration but /var/lib/mariadb is never deleted. Application
configuration changes only after these backups and rehearsal succeed.
Converted dumps are restored with dedicated non-superuser owners; complete row,
constraint and sequence proofs must match again before writers are allowed.

Before native writers start, failures restore source application configurations
and the original system/services. Converted copies are retained for diagnosis.
There is deliberately no overwrite/retry option: review any failed attempt
before touching its restored databases. After native writers start, failures
never automatically roll back, since that could discard new writes.

After correcting a post-writer configuration failure, finish without
reimporting or resetting any data:

  sudo /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/shared-mariadb-migrate \
    --system /etc/nixos/result-native-migration \
    --finish /var/backups/shared-mariadb/<interrupted-timestamp>

Finalization verifies application HTTP health, actual database backend, active
writers, retired services and failed units, then persists the system profile
and boot configuration. Backups and original data remain. Avoid rolling back
to first-stage snapshots after this second stage without accounting for newer
FreshRSS/Panel data.

An unprivileged, source-read-only rehearsal is also available before cutover:

  /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/shared-mariadb-migrate --rehearse

Failures retain private temporary diagnostics. Successful rehearsal cleans up
only its own temporary directory and never changes source applications.

First-stage migration (historical)
--------------------------------

Scope: Audiobookshelf 2.37.2, Forgejo 17.0.1, Uptime Kuma 2.5.9,
standard upstream SearXNG, and the Authentik/Bitmagnet PostgreSQL 16
databases. Other application containers and Pelican/Wings stay unchanged.
Uptime Kuma and FreshRSS still need the existing shared MariaDB container.
Grimmory still needs its separate MariaDB container.
Bazarr, Radarr, and Sonarr were inactive and remain configured for manual start
(autoStart = false), so this cutover does not inadvertently bring them online.

Do not run nixos-rebuild switch before the migration. The native application
units and the retained Authentik/Bitmagnet clients require the migration's
ready marker, so an ordinary rebuild cannot start them against empty databases.

Build the configuration, including new files (path flakes include untracked
files), without updating flake.lock:

  nix build path:/etc/nixos#nixosConfigurations.server.config.system.build.toplevel \
    --out-link /etc/nixos/result-native-migration

Review the changes, then run in an interactive terminal:

  sudo /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/native-services-migrate \
    --system /etc/nixos/result-native-migration

Keep the terminal open. Bitmagnet is approximately 81 GB; full table counts,
dump, restore, verification, and index rebuilding can take a long time.
Migration requires at least three times the current PostgreSQL database size
plus 10 GiB of free space on /var/lib. Backups and originals must not be deleted
to make room during migration. Backups are on the same host, not off-site.

The command refuses existing native data directories and a previous migration
state. It verifies source versions, stops affected writers, and makes root-only
backups under /var/backups/native-services/<timestamp>. These contain secrets.
It preserves the old and new NixOS systems as GC roots. It leaves original
application directories, MariaDB databases, PostgreSQL volume/bind data,
and original environment files untouched.
It checks complete units INCLUDING NixOS drop-ins. Changes to Wings are refused.
Docker/containerd changes require explicit --allow-docker-restart authorization:
even a Docker CLI wrapper path change in an ExecStart drop-in restarts the
daemon and game containers. The engine is not being upgraded here. Locally
cached application images are reused. The shared MariaDB unit waits for TCP
readiness, and Pelican Panel's upgrade runs only after that readiness check.

It copies application state, uses Forgejo's PostgreSQL-format export rather
than loading MariaDB SQL into PostgreSQL, and restores both PostgreSQL dumps
into separate non-superuser roles. It compares all table counts and PostgreSQL
sequence values before allowing clients to start. Audiobookshelf's SQLite
integrity and row counts are checked as well. Media paths remain available
inside native service mount namespaces at their original container locations.
Forgejo's SQL is parsed using PostgreSQL's grammar, not split with regular
expressions. Foreign keys are added and validated after all tables and rows
exist, supporting the forward/cyclic references in its export. The entire
Forgejo SQL import is transactional; no foreign-key checks are disabled.
Forgejo omits its own forgejo_version and forgejo_migration tables from its
export. Their exact source schemas are checked and their rows explicitly
converted too, retaining the migration ledger and avoiding rerunning old
migrations on existing data.

Forgejo retains its complete imported app.ini, including SMTP and encryption
secrets. It is intentionally not declaratively regenerated: edit
/var/lib/forgejo/gitea/conf/app.ini for settings after migration. Its native
built-in SSH server listens on the old published port 222 using the existing
host keys. Its advertised SSH_PORT is preserved from the old configuration.
Only CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE is granted for port 222; the service's private user
namespace is disabled so that capability applies to the host network namespace.
PostgreSQL explicitly disables all TCP listeners, including localhost.

SearXNG intentionally switches from PrivAU to upstream, with a new secret,
standard themes, local Valkey rate limiting, and the same reverse-proxy port.
The PrivAU configuration/cache remains backed up and is not imported.
Its health check uses /healthz; the homepage intentionally subjects automated
requests to bot detection and is not a suitable health probe.

On success the command verifies service health, updates the system profile,
and installs the boot configuration. Daily native PostgreSQL backups retain
the current and previous dumps under /var/backup/postgresql.

Failure and rollback
--------------------

Before native writers start, failures restore the old system automatically.
If health verification fails after writers start, the command leaves the new
system running and reports the failure: automatically rolling back could lose
new writes. Inspect journalctl and the root-only backup state.json.

After correcting configuration and building a new system, finish such a cutover
without copying or restoring any live data again:

  sudo /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/native-services-migrate \
    --system /etc/nixos/result-native-migration \
    --finish /var/backups/native-services/<interrupted-timestamp>

This requires the matching interrupted system and ready marker, checks Docker/
Wings restart safety, activates the corrected system, starts failed writers,
and repeats HTTP, container health, SSH host-key and failed-unit checks before
persisting the system profile and boot configuration. Native data is retained.
Add --allow-docker-restart only if its restart is authorized.

Explicit rollback discards post-cutover changes in the copied applications
and native databases. Confirm that trade-off before running:

  sudo /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/native-services-migrate \
    --rollback /var/backups/native-services/<timestamp> --accept-data-loss

This removes the ready marker, stops new writers and native PostgreSQL, restores
the old system/profile, and starts the original containers against their
untouched data. Native copies and backups remain for investigation.
The repository still contains the native configuration: do not rebuild it
after rollback until the failure has been resolved. Do not remove destination
directories or migration state just to force a retry.

A failure before native writers started can be retried after the original
system and containers are running:

  sudo /etc/nixos/result-native-migration/sw/bin/native-services-migrate \
    --system /etc/nixos/result-native-migration \
    --retry /var/backups/native-services/<failed-timestamp> \
    --allow-docker-restart

Only use the final flag when a Docker/game restart is acceptable. The retry
archives every unfinished native copy and migration state inside the previous
backup's unfinished-native-copies directory; it deletes nothing, takes fresh
backups of the now-current original data, and performs a new migration.

Tests:

  PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m unittest discover \
    -s tests -p test_native_services_migration.py -v
